== no_y
error[Z0401]: `boxplot` draws a box over the values of `y`, but the plot maps no variable to `y`
 --> scripts/main.zeff:3:16
  |
3 | d.plot(x: arm).boxplot()
  |                ^^^^^^^ needs `y`
  |
  = help: map it where the plot starts, as in `plot(x: industry, y: wage).boxplot()` for a box of `wage` for each industry, or `plot(y: wage).boxplot()` for one box

== a_y_of_text
error[Z0401]: `boxplot` draws a box over the values of `y`, but the `y` axis of this plot holds the categories of `name`
 --> scripts/main.zeff:3:25
  |
3 | d.plot(x: arm, y: name).boxplot()
  |                         ^^^^^^^ needs numbers along `y`
  |
  = help: `points` and `lines` are placed on an axis of categories; `boxplot` needs a number along `y`

== a_y_of_dates
error[Z0401]: `boxplot` draws a box over the values of `y`, but the `y` axis of this plot holds the dates of `day`
 --> scripts/main.zeff:3:24
  |
3 | d.plot(x: arm, y: day).boxplot()
  |                        ^^^^^^^ needs numbers along `y`
  |
  = help: `points`, `lines` and `step` are placed on a scale of dates; `boxplot` needs a number along `y`, and may have dates along the other axis

== outliers_that_are_a_number
error[Z0401]: `outliers` of `boxplot` must be a Bool, but this is an Int
 --> scripts/main.zeff:3:36
  |
3 | d.plot(y: score).boxplot(outliers: 1)
  |                                    ^ an Int
  |
  = help: usage: `p.boxplot(data: Frame, outliers: Bool = true)`

== outliers_that_are_a_word
