 --> scripts/main.zeff:3:36
  |
3 | d.plot(y: score).boxplot(outliers: none)
  |                                    ^^^^ not a binding

== a_box_plot_of_a_frame
error[Z0401]: `boxplot` cannot be called on a Frame
 --> scripts/main.zeff:3:1
  |
3 | d.boxplot()
  | ^ this is a Frame
  |
  = help: `boxplot` takes a Plot first; `x.boxplot(...)` is `boxplot(x, ...)`

== a_fill_on_a_ramp
error[Z0401]: `boxplot` fills each area in one colour, so `fill` must show categories, but `score` is an Int without value labels, which is shown on a continuous scale
 --> scripts/main.zeff:3:39
  |
3 | d.plot(x: arm, y: score, fill: score).boxplot()
  |                                       ^^^^^^^ a colour for each row, not for each area
  |
  = help: fill by a variable of categories, as in `boxplot(fill: union)`, or set one fill for this layer, as in `boxplot(fill: "steelblue")`

== a_frame_without_the_variable
error[Z0101]: no variable `score` in `the frame`
 --> scripts/main.zeff:3:40
  |
3 | d.plot(x: arm, y: score).boxplot(data: frame(arm = ["a"], other = [1]))
  |                          ------- this layer draws `score`, which the plot maps to `y`
  |                                        ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^ not a variable of the frame
  |
  = help: the variables of `the frame` are: arm, other
